# Minecraft Version(s): 26.1–26.3
## 1.13.2
- /pets on and /pets off: take your pet out or put it away quickly (on brings back the one you had out last)

## 1.13.1
- Pets:
  - the head faced backwards; it looks the right way now
  - stays right with you when you run, sprint or fly (before, it fell behind)
  - /pets is simpler: your pets with Take out / Put away on the front page, and buttons for Moves and Particles that count for whichever pet is out
  - seven more ways to move: Shoulder, Lazy, Peek, Halo, Spiral, Figure eight and Wander (twelve in all)
  - particle trails that rise off the pet: Wisps (end rod), Hearts, Notes, Flames, Soul flames, Snow, Glow, Cherry petals, Magic, Sparks, Fireflies, Bubbles (can be switched off for everyone in the panel)
  - a pet's new name in the panel now reaches the players who have it, like its head did
  - pets taken out of the shop stay listed in the panel while players have them, so their name and head can still be changed
  - the player card in Players → Players shows the player's pets: give one (free, for days or forever), add time, make one permanent, take it out or put it away, change how it moves and its particles, or take it away
- Market and pet shop menus look tidier: clickable [ Buy ] / [ View ] labels instead of underlined names, the facts of an item in one line, and a cleaner item page (it no longer says "You have free" when you carry no diamonds)
- Claiming land too far out says so plainly: "You can't claim here: land this far out is reset from time to time."

## 1.13.0
- Pets: floating player heads that follow their owner, only for looks (no effects, can't be hit or pushed), and every player nearby sees them (normal game clients work)
  - /pets: your pets: take one out, put it away, and pick how it moves: float next to your shoulder, orbit your head, bounce, spin above your head or follow behind you
  - Pet shop (in /pets and as Pets in /market): pets set up in the panel with a name, a head texture, a price in the market's currency (or free), how long they last (or forever) and how many can be sold (or no limit); buying a timed pet again adds more time, and a first pet comes out right away
  - Pets that run out disappear with a message; pets hide while their owner is invisible or spectating
  - Permission nicecontrolcenter.pets.use (everyone by default)
  - New API /api/pets/state for the panel

## 1.12.3
- Tool wear multipliers fixed:
  - silk touch spawners only ever took the normal 1 point, because the game wears the tool after the break event;
  - tree felling and vein mining now also scale the wear of the block you break yourself (before, only the extra logs and ores were scaled, so a small tree or vein barely showed the multiplier).

## 1.12.2
- @Mentions also work in messages sent from the panel's Chat tab: the mentioned player hears the plop, and the mention is coloured when that's switched on
- Bone meal on more plants shows the green sparkles like vanilla bone meal (before, only the sound came)
- Silk touch spawners: which tools may pick spawners up can be set (pickaxes by default), and Silk Touch can be made optional

## 1.12.1
- Tool wear multipliers for tree felling, vein mining and silk touch spawners go up to 100 (was 10)

## 1.12.0
- New gameplay tweaks (all off until switched on in the panel, normal game clients work):
  - Grindstone to books: an enchanted item and a book give an enchanted book with the item's enchantments (curses stay on the item), for levels (per enchantment level, with a least and a most); the price shows above the hotbar
  - Silk touch spawners: a spawner mined with Silk Touch drops as an item that keeps its mob and places again for normal players; mobs can be excluded, with a tool wear multiplier and optional XP
  - Saplings plant themselves: saplings lying on the ground plant themselves after a set time where they can grow, keeping room around them and (optionally) off claimed land
  - Bone meal on more plants: sugar cane, cactus (up to a set height), nether wart, lily pads, vines and small flowers
  - Loyalty tridents come back from the void
  - @Mentions in chat: the mentioned player hears the plop and sees who mentioned them (also from Discord); the mention can be coloured
- Tree felling and vein mining share their tool wear code with spawners

## 1.11.2
- Tab list header and footer update every second instead of every 5 seconds, so {time}, {tps} and {ping} keep up

## 1.11.1
- Nether portals in any shape use the same frame blocks as normal portals: on 26.3 the block tag minecraft:nether_portal_frame (so blocks a data pack adds to it work for custom shapes too), on 26.1 and 26.2 obsidian

## 1.11.0
- Anvil: "never Too Expensive" now charges the full cost however high it is (it used to lower it to a limit). Game clients can't show more than 39, so the anvil shows 39 and the real price appears above the hotbar; taking the result checks and charges the real price
- Tree felling: the tree tips over away from the player and falls (block displays, seen by normal clients); its logs, crown and drops land where it falls. Can be switched off
- Tree felling and vein mining: tool wear per extra block can be set (1 = normal, 0.5 = half, 0 = none, 2 = double)
- Felling and vein mining show the breaking of every block to the player too
- Tab list: an empty line between the header/footer and the players, and optionally each player's dimension after their name, with a name and colour per dimension
- Market: Browse shows every listing with its item icon and the item's own tooltip (enchantments, lore…); click a name to buy. Search, order and pages are above the listings, Back is its own button at the bottom (also in Sell, My listings, Mailbox and the listing itself). My listings and the mailbox show icons too
- Claim and market messages come with the little chicken-egg "plop", like the Explorer's Eden data packs

## 1.10.0
- Gameplay tweaks, all set in the panel (Tweaks) and working with normal game clients:
  - Anvil: never "Too Expensive!" (costs over the limit, 39 by default, are lowered to it) and cheap renaming (1 level by default)
  - Nether portals in any shape: any closed obsidian frame (circles, arches…) lights, up to a size limit, and stays lit while the frame is whole; rectangles work as always
  - Tree felling like FallingTree: break one log with an axe and the whole tree comes down (all at once or one log per hit from the top). Sneaking breaks just one log, the axe wears per log and stops before it breaks, logs placed without leaves around stay single blocks, leaves fall within seconds, nether wart blocks and mangrove roots go with their trees, optional drops at the first log, size limits, tool/log/leaf lists
  - Vein mining: the same for ores with a pickaxe (deepslate and normal ore are one vein)
  - Every felled or mined block is broken as if the player mined it: drops, Fortune and Silk Touch, XP, tool wear and land claims work as usual
  - AFK tag in the tab list (scoreboard teams, like Fabled Roots' races, are left alone) and a tab list header/footer with players, TPS, ping and the in-game clock
- The market (/market): players sell items for the server's currency (any amount, more than a stack is fine, all components kept) and buy part or all of a listing, in dialogs. Earnings and unsold items (after the listing time) wait in the market mailbox; sellers are told when something sells
- For other mods (Merl): the server's rules in plain words (performance tweaks, borders, claims, gameplay tweaks, the market) and the claims a player owns or is a member of

## 1.9.3
- Discord bridge (with the panel): messages data packs send to everyone with /tellraw @a, and /say and /me, go to the panel as their own event (posted when switched on in the panel)
- Fabled Roots' death messages count as deaths on Discord, with the pack's own English text (data packs' language files are read for that)

## 1.9.2
- Claims switched on or off in the panel apply right away even while the server is paused with nobody online (before, claims stayed off on the map until someone joined or the server restarted)

## 1.9.1
- Claims: minecarts with chests or hoppers and chest boats can only be opened by members with the containers role (or with the claim's switch on); before, anyone could open them
- The switch "open chests and use animals" is now "open chests and interact with entities": it always covered villagers, interaction entities and other mobs too

## 1.9.0
- Land claims, like GriefPrevention but with menus: players claim land with the claim tool (two corners), and everything inside is protected from bedrock to the sky. Off until switched on in the panel; GOML keeps working alongside
- Claim mode (/claim mode or the menu) hands out the claim tool, a marked golden hoe, and takes it away again. It can't be dropped, stored or kept; whatever was in the hand comes back
- The /claim menu (dialogs, no client mod): your claim blocks and claims. Per claim: members with a role (visitor, containers, builder, co-owner), what everyone else may do (doors, chests, PvP, explosions, fire, mob griefing), name and colour, subclaims with their own members, abandoning. Shortcuts: /claim list, trust, untrust, abandon; /claim admin bypass, delete, blocks
- Claim blocks: a start amount and more per hour of play (not while AFK), bonus blocks from admins, and an optional shop in the menu that sells blocks for an item
- Claims only inside the permanent area (soft border) and the allowed dimensions; minimum size, claims per player and removing claims of long-gone players are settings
- Borders show as fake blocks only the player sees: corners, markers along the edges, subclaims and the first corner. The blocks are settings; claim_color uses the concrete closest to the claim's colour
- Protected: building and breaking, doors, buttons, chests and other storage, buckets, animals, villagers, armor stands, frames and vehicles. Also players hurting animals (arrows too) and each other (PvP switch), arrows setting off blocks (targets, bells, buttons, TNT) or breaking frames, explosions (blocks, and animals, frames and armor stands inside), fire spread and lava lighting fires, water and lava flowing in, pistons pushing in, endermen, the wither, ravagers, zombies breaking doors and trampling farmland
- Claims are kept in the world folder (nicecontrolcenter-claims.json); the panel reads, edits and copies them through the dashboard API, and they show on the web map in their colour

## 1.8.0
- Fix: with the activation range on, mobs far from players stopped ticking entirely instead of every few ticks (a skipped mob's own tick count doesn't move; the turn now follows the world's clock)
- Mob limits per chunk: pick mobs or groups (all animals, all monsters, villagers, water mobs) and how many may live in one chunk; used by the cleanup tool, the schedule's cleanup step and the new auto caps, which apply them every few seconds (optionally also a dropped item limit, oldest first). Named, tamed, leashed and persistent mobs are never removed
- Mob pushing cap (Paper's max-entity-collisions): a mob pushes at most N others per tick, and the search for them stops early; cramming damage unchanged. 150 cows in a pen: 4.7 → 2.9 ms per tick
- Junk despawns sooner: chosen items (cobblestone, netherrack, rotten flesh…) after e.g. 60 seconds; arrows mobs shot after 10 seconds
- Save limits per chunk (Paper's entity-per-chunk-save-limit): only N arrows, XP orbs, snowballs… are written when a chunk saves
- Lighter mob AI: far from players, mobs re-plan their goals only every few ticks (Pufferfish's goal throttle); while lagging, a limit on new path searches per tick
- Idle hoppers wait a few ticks before looking again (moving and locked hoppers unchanged)
- Armor stands that stand still tick once a second (Paper's armor-stands-tick: false). 400 stands: 7.3 → 3.6 ms
- Lag protection has two more steps before the view distance: activation ranges halved, then entities shown from 60 % of the distance
- Chunks loaded without players (forceload, ender pearls, portals, dragon) with why, whether they tick and what's there, and Release
- Players table: measured tick time around each player (each chunk counted for the nearest player), their share of the total and their costliest chunk; sorted by it

## 1.7.3
- The panel's Commands page: every command with the mod that adds it, the lowest operator level that may use it, its permission node (fabric-permissions-api checks) and how it's written
- Commands run in the panel's console can be shown to online operators, grey and italic like vanilla's notices ("[Admin ┃ NiceRon: /time set day]"); switched on in the panel

## 1.7.2
- The changes of 1.7.1 under a new number, so panels install them: "Admin ┃ <account>: …" for messages from the panel (1.7.1 was rebuilt after its first release, and panels only update to higher numbers)
- The dashboard on its own gets the panel's look: brand glow and block grid, each page in its colour (tabs and card icons), gradient buttons

## 1.7.1
- Item tooltips on the Players page show names and lore with their colours and styles (§ codes and coloured text no longer show as plain text); lore shows like in the game
- Player cards show when the player first joined and when they were last online (or "online since"); kept in the world folder (nicecontrolcenter-seen.json), with an estimate from file dates for players from before
- GOML claims show on the map (and player cards) within seconds: they're read from GOML's memory instead of the saved world, which only changed on the next world save; both of GOML's save formats are understood
- Messages from the panel read "Admin ┃ <name>: …" with the sender's panel account, private ones and chat to everyone alike (chat no longer goes out as /say); message_prefix takes {user}; the old defaults "[Admin]" and "Admin:" move to the new one, a prefix you set yourself stays
- Waypoint names and descriptions on the Players page are read correctly when stored as text components

## 1.7.0
- Performance tools in the spirit of Paper and ServerCore, set up in the panel (config/nicecontrolcenter-performance.json, applied live):
  - Dynamic distances: while ticks take too long, the simulation distance, then the mob caps, then the view distance step down; they go back up once the server is calm
  - Entity activation range: mobs far from every player tick only every few ticks (never falling, swimming, fighting, burning, leashed, riding or raiding ones)
  - Larger merge radius for dropped items and XP, breeding caps for animals and villagers, villagers stuck in trading cells think less often
  - Frozen chunks: nothing ticks in them until they're unfrozen
- Cleanup on demand: dropped items and XP, or mobs over a per-chunk cap (never named, tamed, leashed or persistent ones), with a preview of what would go
- Maintenance mode (config/nicecontrolcenter-maintenance.json): only operators and staff can join, its own server list message and icon (only in the list: server.properties is left alone), a countdown in chat before everyone else is kicked
- The in-game date and time (Nice Actions calendar) is sent along with the map data, for the panel's public map
- The soft border shows as green particles to players near it, a round hard border as red ones ("Show soft border" in the panel's borders)
- Players put back from past the hard border bring their vehicle (and whoever rides it), leashed animals and following pets
- Waypoint names and descriptions stored as text components are read correctly for the map, with the owner's UUID for their head
- Charts use the Inter font

## 1.6.0
- Pregeneration carries on after a restart: the running job and the queue are kept in config/nicecontrolcenter-pregen.json and pick up at the ring they were working on (Stop clears them)
- Pregeneration can slow down (default) or pause while players are online, or keep full speed
- Lag alerts also go to the panel (for its Discord alerts)

## 1.5.0
- Pregeneration runs on its own thread instead of in the server tick: it only queues chunks for Minecraft's world-generation threads, sleeps until one is done and still backs off when ticks get long; counting a huge area no longer holds up the server
- Pregeneration can skip an inner area (only the outer wilds) and several dimensions can be queued one after another
- Borders per dimension (config/nicecontrolcenter-borders.json, set in the panel's World tab): a soft border around the permanent area and a hard border nobody gets past; players past the hard border (walking, flying, pearls, portals) are put back where they last stood inside, circles included; a square hard border also sets that dimension's world border
- New Disk tile with the stat tiles: size of the world, of the whole server folder and free space on the drive (measured every 2 minutes)
- Pregeneration holds each chunk with its own ticket until it's fully generated; before, a few percent of chunks could be unloaded half-way and counted as failed
- The GC tile also shows how busy the collector is in the background ("busy N%"); concurrent collectors like ZGC barely pause, so with a nearly full heap the tile showed 0% paused while the server was waiting for memory. It turns gold or red then, and the memory pressure finding says so

## 1.4.5
- New keep_reports_days setting (default 14): recordings and reports older than that are deleted at server start, after each new report and once an hour; 0 keeps them forever

## 1.4.4
- Dashboard links are renewed at every server start by default; the new token_hours setting keeps a link working for that many hours instead (also across restarts), then makes a new one
- The stat cards are back to their previous look and sit in a second row of the pinned top bar (one row that scrolls sideways on phones)
- The Explorer's Eden icon for home-screen shortcuts (apple-touch-icon), like on the website

## 1.4.3
- Item tooltips in the player inspector show right away when hovering, drawn like the game's (name, enchantments, item id)
- The stat tiles keep following the server live when the browser's clock differs from the server's (another machine or a container); before, they could fall back to slower window values

## 1.4.2
- The stat tiles (TPS, MSPT, CPU, memory, GC, entities, block entities, chunks, players) are pinned in the top bar and stay in view while scrolling, also inside the panel; details are in each tile's tooltip, and less important tiles step aside on narrower screens
- The live badge only shows the connection state (Live, paused, disconnected), no TPS

## 1.4.1
- The Chat tab is a copy of the in-game chat: everything players get in their chat window, with Minecraft's colors and formatting (bold, italic, underline, links), including tellraw, /msg, command feedback, joins, leaves, deaths, advancements and Discord messages
- A message sent to several players at once is one line; messages only some players got show who got them (→ names) and can be hidden with "Private messages"
- Messages to everyone while nobody is online (/say, Discord) are kept too

## 1.4.0
- New Chat tab in the dashboard: player chat, /say and /me, joins, leaves, deaths and advancements, and messages from Discord (Discord: JustSync, or the panel's Discord bridge), with filters, search and a box to send a message to everyone (as /say)
- The console no longer has its own Chat filter; chat lives in the Chat tab

## 1.3.3
- The stat tiles always show the server right now: MSPT as min / median / 95%ile / max of the last 10 seconds, GC over the last 10 seconds, and no window averages underneath
- The stat tiles are on every tab, not only the Overview

## 1.3.2
- The player inspector's list fills the window height and stays in view while a long player detail scrolls

## 1.3.1
- Pregeneration no longer waits for each chunk on the server thread (a large area could stall a tick until the watchdog stopped the server); chunks are now requested in the background, about 250 per second on a fast machine
- Pregeneration keeps the server awake without writing pause-when-empty-seconds to server.properties, so a crash during a run can't leave it at 0
- Updates from GitHub (update_github) pick the release built for the server's Minecraft version instead of GitHub's "latest" release, which could be a jar for another version

## 1.3.0
- One jar each for Minecraft 26.3, 26.2 and 26.1 (26.1, 26.1.1 and 26.1.2)
- The stat tiles size to their content: MSPT and Memory get more room than the single numbers, so nothing is cut off (one row on wide screens, two rows in between, two per row on phones)
- With the panel: pregeneration of a square or circle (Chunky-style spiral, only as fast as the server has room for, keeps going while the server would otherwise pause when empty)
- With the panel: the web map's overlays (players, world border, GOML claims, waypoint hubs) and the map colors of all blocks, modded ones included

## 1.2.0
- With the Nice Control Center Panel: the Discord bridge's login check (players need a linked Discord account when the panel asks for it) and chat, join, leave, death and advancement messages for Discord
- "Error sending packet clientbound/minecraft:disconnect" and "handleDisconnection() called twice" are marked as harmless (connections closing during a server-list ping or a failed login)

## 1.1.0
- Works with the Nice Control Center Panel (Docker): when the panel runs the server, the dashboard listens only inside the container, the panel shows it in its Dashboard tab with its own accounts and permissions, and /ncc web links open the panel
- The dashboard follows the panel's light/dark switch and drops its own header when shown inside the panel

## 1.0.6
- The live TPS badge in the header keeps its width when the value changes (e.g. 20.0 → 19.9)

## 1.0.5
- The TPS, MSPT, CPU, Memory, entity, chunk and player tiles show the current second (updated every second) instead of the chosen time window; the window's average is the small line underneath. GC stays a window value
- Charts scroll smoothly out at the left edge instead of the oldest part being cut off

## 1.0.4
- Answers to admin messages pop up as a notification on every tab (click to open the conversation), with the count in the browser tab title
- Player inspector: the last grave shows who looted it (highlighted if it wasn't the owner) or that it expired, instead of just "removed"

## 1.0.3
- The Memory tile is laid out like MSPT: in use after GC, with garbage not collected yet (both color-coded), and the maximum

## 1.0.2
- \n typed into the MOTD in the server.properties editor is saved as a real second line instead of the text "\n"
- New GC tile next to Memory: share of time paused for garbage collection, number of pauses and the average pause
- "Close to its limit" only shows when more than 2% of ticks run over budget, not because of a handful of slow ticks (autosaves) while the server keeps up easily

## 1.0.1
- Packs installed as mods show their real name in data pack settings and elsewhere (Warping Wonders instead of "wawo")
- Data pack settings also find packs that keep their settings in their own storage, like Katters Structures
- The server name in the dashboard and in reports shows the MOTD's colors and formatting instead of the raw § codes

## First release
- Live monitor that starts with the server: TPS, MSPT (min/med/95%ile/max), CPU, memory, entities, chunks and players for the last 1, 5, 15 and 60 minutes
- "What's costing performance": plain-language findings with the cause and a tip, all in ms per tick
- Data pack functions timed down to single command lines, mods found with a stack sampler, entity and block entity types, busiest chunks and lag spikes with their causes
- World generation cost per step, structure and feature, grouped by data pack or mod
- Player view, redstone clock finder, settings advisor and bloat check
- Lag alerts in chat and automatic lag reports, plus manual recordings as shareable HTML reports and a before/after comparison
- Data pack settings from in-game settings menus, editable in the dashboard (same options and limits as the menu)
- In-game date and time in the dashboard header (Nice Actions calendar or the Minecraft day counter)
- Web dashboard in the Explorer's Eden style with light and dark mode, /ncc commands, an in-game boss bar and LuckPerms permission nodes
- Errors & warnings from the server log, grouped and credited to data packs and mods, with a chat message after a /reload that broke something
- Console in the dashboard: live log and a command line
- Automatic updates for mods and data packs from Modrinth and GitHub, installed at restart, with backups and rollback (/ncc updates)
- Dashboard split into tabs
- Server resource pack kept up to date from a link, GitHub or Modrinth, with the SHA-1 worked out by the mod
- server.properties editor in the dashboard
- Live player inspector: online and offline players, inventories, homes, graves, waypoints and claims
- Gamerule editor in the dashboard
- Data pack settings laid out like the packs' in-game menus, with search
- In-game settings screen through Mod Menu (singleplayer)
- Smoother live charts and in-game clock; calmer layout with a compact header on every tab but the Overview
- Scheduled commands: once, daily, on weekdays or every few minutes, several commands per task
- Private messages with players (they answer with /nccreply), ban and unban in the player inspector
- Scheduled tasks can be chained (run after another task, with a delay), and commands can be spaced out with wait lines or a pause
- Player inspector shows each player's client and recognised mods, and an x-ray hint from mining statistics
